  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nThe upper-level pattern is currently comprised of broad ridging \nover the western CONUS and a trough positioned over the eastern \nCONUS. The tropics are currently fairly active with 2 Pacific \nstorms, Rachel to the north and Simon to the south (more on \nSimon later in the discussion), and Hurricane Isaias in the \nGulf. At the surface, a weak low-pressure system is moving \nnortheast across NE with a surface boundary lifting north \nthrough central IA and northeastern MO. On the warm (south) side\nof the boundary, 500-1000 J/kg of MUCAPE is present in \nconjunction with 30 kts of deep layer shear. Storms have \ninitiated across central IA along this surface boundary and at \nthe nose of a 25-30 kt LLJ and have been producing marginally \nsevere hail over the past couple of hours (roughly 10p-1a). \nStorm motions are generally southeast, which is parallel to the \nboundary (and MUCAPE gradient). As storms approach the MO/IA \nborder, they will be moving into a less favorable environment \nwith less support for storm longevity. While the current \nexpectation is for storms to stay north and east of the CWA, the\nMUCAPE gradient is currently situated through the far \nnortheastern corner of the CWA, so precipitation cannot be fully\nruled out, but severe weather is not expected to continue south\nof the MO/IA border.\n\nThe unseasonable warmth that has been present over the past\ncouple of days is expected to continue with temperatures this \nmorning only expected to fall into the low 60s. The \naforementioned ridge over the western CONUS will slide east \ntoday bringing mid-level height rises to the Central Plains \nsupportive of temperatures in the mid-to-upper 80s. Areas in \ncentral MO may even make a run at 90F. These values are 15-20 \ndegrees above what is normal for this time of year (normal is a \nhigh of 70F in Kansas City, 68F in Kirksville, and 70F in \nSedalia). These temperatures will persist through the weekend \nand into early next week, thanks to the ridge stalling in its \neastward progression as Isaias gets absorbed into the flow. \nAside from some high-level clouds across central MO, the impacts\nof Isaias will stay to the east of the CWA and the forecast \nremains dry.\n\nModel guidance diverges Tuesday through the end of the week\nbringing uncertainty to the forecast. Hurricane Simon is\nforecast to make landfall along the west coast of Mexico (near\nGuadalajara) Saturday night into Sunday morning. The moisture\nassociated with this system will then continue north and\neventually make its way into the Central Plains by mid-week. \nHowever, there are discrepancies as to when exactly that plume \narrives. The quicker solutions (deterministic GFS and ECMWF and \nthe European ensemble) have the moisture arriving Tuesday with \nprecipitation chances increasing Tuesday afternoon/evening, \nwhile the slower solutions (GEFS and Canadian ensemble) bring \nthe moisture into the area on Wednesday. When this remnant \nmoisture actually arrives will dictate temperatures Tuesday and\nWednesday with the slower solution yielding less cloud cover \nand warmer temperatures, while the faster solution points to \ncooler temperatures and increased precipitation chances. LREF \nguidance currently indicates that there is a 40-60% chance of \nexceeding 80F on Tuesday and Wednesday with a ~15 degree spread\nfrom the 25th to 75th percentiles (73F vs 88F respectively for \nthe KC Metro). To summarize all of this, there is a plume of \nmoisture that is expected to overspread the Central Plains mid- \nweek next week. When it arrives, precipitation chances will \nincrease to 50-70%. Until it does arrive, temperatures will \nremain 15-20 degrees above normal.",
